@page "/not-found"
@page "/{**path:nonfile}"
@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
@layout MainLayout
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

<BlogPageTitle Title="Not found" />
@* WORKAROUND(W-008, Blazor): HeadOutlet keeps one HeadContent provider per page, so the feed link sits beside RobotsNoIndex here *@
<HeadContent><RobotsNoIndex /><FeedAlternateLink /></HeadContent>
@* END WORKAROUND(W-008) *@

@* Spec §5.5: the themed not-found page, a static document like the reading pages (brief 13a): an
   anonymous visitor who follows a dead link must not open a circuit. Under a staff path an
   interactive page that calls NotFound() still renders it in its circuit (the staff variant of
   NotFoundContent). This page has two jobs:
   - it is the Router's NotFoundPage, rendered when a matched page calls NavigationManager.NotFound()
     (missing post, unknown category/tag slug);
   - its catch-all route catches URLs no @page route matches, which never reach the Blazor Router at
     all (the framework maps one endpoint per page), and turns them into a real 404 the same way.
   The catch-all binds {**path}: Path is non-null exactly when the catch-all matched. Rendered as the
   Router's NotFoundPage (or via /not-found) Path is null and no NotFound() call is made — /not-found
   must keep answering 200 so a redirect to it is distinguishable from a real 404. *@
<NotFoundContent />

@code {
    [Parameter] public string? Path { get; set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        if (Path is not null)
        {
            // WORKAROUND(W-024, Blazor): NavigateTo("/not-found") answers 200; NavigationManager.NotFound() makes the renderer set 404
            // Real 404 (spec §5.5): the Router renders this page's NotFoundContent and the endpoint
            // renderer sets the HTTP status; never NavigateTo("/not-found"), which redirects with 200.
            Navigation.NotFound();
            // END WORKAROUND(W-024)
        }
    }
}
